now I know what you're thinking hang on  the Soviet Union had elections but  wasn't it a one-party state well yes but  that didn't mean that you couldn't vote  and the USSR had many elections open to  the wider Soviet public now the nature  of these votes did change over the  decades and as such we're going to focus  on the years when the USSR was run by  Joseph Stalin since he's not really one  you would associate with democracy so  citizens of the USSR elected  representatives to the Supreme Soviet  the legislative arm of the government  and these elections were held every four  years and the process for these  elections was fairly simple for citizens  when polling day arrived they would go  to their local polling station and they  would receive their ballot which would  only have one candidate on it so most  people the fact that you could only pick  from one Communist Party approved  candidate doesn't seem very democratic  and that's because it isn't however  these elections weren't without their  purpose and in some ways it did give  ordinary Soviet citizens a voice in how  the USSR was run before that it's  important to say how candidates were  selected and what this meant for how the  USSR operated after these elections  obviously you couldn't get onto the  ballot without the express consent of  the Communist Party and the vast  majority of candidates were Communist  Party ones some were independent  candidates which gives a nice bit of  variation providing you ignore the fact  that they were also communists and would  agree with the Communist Party on  everything also in case you're wondering  the reason why all candidates stood  unopposed was that the party wouldn't  allow itself to appear divided and just  so you know here's how the voting  process would work one you'd turn up at  the polling station two you'd receive a  ballot that would have the name of your  candidate on it three you'd place their  ballot in the ballot box and that would  be counted as a vote for the candidate  but what if a person didn't wish to vote  for the candidate on the paper well then  they were free to enter the voting booth  and scribble out the candidates name but  then everyone would know that you did it  because you were one of the few that  kept their ballot a secret so with  elections being run in this way then  surely there was no point of them will  actually know for the state they were  used to demonstrate popular support for  the Communist Party in its policies high  turnout meant that it was widely popular  which you could use to then demonstrate  to the rest of the world that I had  popular support this is why the  government went to such lengths to get  people to vote and if he were on weld  they'd bring the ballot box to you so  how did these elections benefit ordinary  people well here's the thing communist  candidates could fail to be elected  despite it being a one horse race you  see if a candidate failed to get 50% of  their areas vote be that from spoiled  ballots or low turnout then they weren't  elected and  a new candidate was brought forward  knowing this Soviet citizens would often  band together and make petitions for  local changes and if they weren't met no  one went out to vote thus if said  representative wanted to keep their nice  political job they had to do something  so in essence Soviet democracy wasn't  really a democracy but it would be wrong  to say that these elections were nothing  more than a farce in a waste of time  Soviet citizens were smart about using  them to get local improvements that they  needed it may not have been particularly  democratic since you couldn't vote the  Communist Party out of office but it did  in a very small sense give you an input  into how things were run I hope you  enjoyed this episode and thank you for  watching but thanks to my patreon  supporters whom you can see on screen  now plus with an extra special thanks to  James Bissonnette Danny Maloney coolin  Castleman 